</TabItem>
</Tabs>

Stage only intended source/test files by their actual paths. Inspect `git diff --cached` before committing through the team's normal branch/PR process. Required peer reviews, Danger status, coverage gates, and Bitrise checks still apply.

CI runs the committed tests through the existing workflow. No workflow change is needed merely because an assistant helped author a test. A new framework, target, fixture mechanism, or device integration still needs its actual configuration.

| Work | Where it runs | What must remain consistent |
|---|---|---|
| Draft a test | Approved Copilot/ChatGPT authoring environment. | Permitted context and existing project conventions. |
| Focused unit/store test | Your Mac, then the configured Bitrise test workflow. | Actual test discovery, assertions, and exit status. |
| Full coverage | Configured local/CI coverage commands. | Complete production scope, fresh reports, and 100% for supported metrics. |
| Native device journey | Local device target and approved Sauce Labs integration. | Matching app/test packages, real selectors, fixtures, and completed suite results. |
| Release progression | Approved CI/CD routes. | Required review, security, signing, and destination verification. |

Use [Coverage & Quality Gates](/mobile/testing-quality/coverage-and-quality-gates/) for enforcement, [Sauce Labs Device Testing](/mobile/cicd/sauce-labs-real-device-testing/) for packaging and the exact integration YAML, and [Danger PR Checks](/mobile/cicd/danger-pr-checks/) for PR feedback. Keep each configuration in its owning guide.

**Attach screenshot — reviewed test diff and passing local result, with sensitive code/data hidden.**

## 7. Set up the workflow once per team

| Step | Action / expected result |
| :--- | :--- |
| 1 | Establish and commit the actual base/lifecycle helpers once per platform. Reuse existing helpers; do not create fictitious classes to satisfy a prompt. |
| 2 | Establish Page Objects/selectors for the real chicken selection, quantity, submit, and confirmation controls. |
| 3 | Review the short `conventions.md` with the team. |
| 4 | Walk one developer through a focused prompt using permitted context and the known order scenario. |
| 5 | Review/run the test and execute the approved Bitrise → Sauce path; inspect actual device failures and evidence. |
| 6 | Reuse the verified infrastructure for subsequent behavior tests, with new explicit acceptance criteria. |

The assistant drafts structure. The developer owns behavior, imports, selectors, credentials/context, assertions, and validity. Platform/QA owners retain integration, device-matrix, protected-input, and evidence responsibilities.

If a draft depends on unavailable product/API or runner contracts, record the missing input rather than invent a working implementation. No AI prompt or green generated test replaces the real product specification.

