4.3 Provisioning config (.provision.env, on your laptop only, gitignored)
Template: .provision.env.example. Holds the two API tokens plus the secret values:
* BITRISE_TOKEN: Bitrise -> Account settings -> Security -> Personal access tokens
* BITRISE_APP_SLUG: Your app URL: app.bitrise.io/app/<APP_SLUG>
* BITRISE_WEBHOOK_URL: Bitrise -> App settings -> Integrations -> Webhooks -> GitLab URL
* GITLAB_HOST: git.enterprise.com
* GITLAB_TOKEN: git.enterprise.com -> Preferences -> Access tokens -> scope api
* GITLAB_PROJECT: group/subgroup/repo path, or numeric project ID
* DEFAULT_BRANCH: Optional, auto-detected
* GITLAB_WEBHOOK_SECRET: Optional shared secret for the webhook
* TEST_PASSWORD, SAUCE_USERNAME, SAUCE_ACCESS_KEY, DEMO_AUTH_USERNAME: from ERPM / Sauce Labs
* TEAMS_WEBHOOK_URL, TEAMS_ENABLED: Optional, from Teams admin
The file is sourced by bash. Wrap any value containing spaces, quotes, $, or \ in single quotes.

4.4 Test credentials — what username and password to use
1. At build time, scripts/write-build-env.sh writes TEST_PASSWORD into the app
   (src/config/buildEnv.ts) as the only password the login screen accepts.
2. At test time, the login test types testuser01 + TEST_PASSWORD.
3. Same secret in both places, so they always match. To change the password, change
   the Bitrise Secret. The next build bakes in the new value.
Set the same value in Bitrise Secrets (TEST_PASSWORD), your local .env / .vault/secrets.env,
and type it by hand if you log in manually on the device. testuser01 is not the password.
For production APIs, use a dedicated test account from ERPM. Put its username in
DEMO_AUTH_USERNAME and its password in TEST_PASSWORD.

