```text
================================================================================
5. STEP-BY-STEP SETUP
================================================================================
Do the steps in order. Each step ends with a Check so you know it worked before moving on.

Step 1 — Get the automation files into your repo (~15 min)
If your app is React Native (Android/iOS), you need two things: the pipeline files
(copied from the reference app) and the test framework (installed as a package).

1a. Copy the pipeline files from mobile-procurement-app into your repo root:
    bitrise.yml
    saucelabs_request.yml / saucelabs_request.ios.yml
    .provision.env.example
    .vault/secrets.env.example
    .gitignore (merge the "Secrets" section into yours)
    scripts/ (whole folder, including scripts/provision/ and notify-teams.sh)
    src/config/buildEnv.ts (empty-string placeholder only — never real values)

1b. Install the test framework (do not copy tests/base or tests/pages from reference app):
    # from Enterprise Git, pinned to a tag:
    npm i -D git+https://git.enterprise.com/<framework-group>/enterprise-mobile-test-framework.git#rn-appium-v1.1.0
    # WebdriverIO peer dependencies:
    npm i -D @wdio/cli @wdio/local-runner @wdio/mocha-framework @wdio/sauce-service @wdio/spec-reporter @wdio/junit-reporter webdriverio appium

    Then copy the package templates into your project:
    templates/wdio.conf.ts       -> tests/wdio.conf.ts (one line: export const config = createConfig({ specs: [...] });)
    templates/pages/*.ts         -> tests/pages/ (example page objects extending BasePage)
    templates/specs/login.test.ts -> tests/login/login.test.ts (known-good example)
    templates/.env.example       -> .env (gitignored)
    conventions.md               -> tests/conventions.md

1c. Add the scripts to your package.json:
    "scripts": {
      "test:unit": "jest",
      "test:e2e": "wdio run tests/wdio.conf.ts",
      "test:e2e:verify": "tsc --noEmit -p tests/tsconfig.json",
      "lint": "bash scripts/lint.sh",
      "security": "bash scripts/security-scan.sh",
      "pipeline:local": "bash scripts/run-pipeline-local.sh",
      "bootstrap": "bash scripts/bootstrap-dev.sh",
      "provision": "bash scripts/provision/provision.sh",
      "provision:apply": "bash scripts/provision/provision.sh --apply",
      "notify:test": "bash scripts/notify-teams.sh --test",
      "notify:preview": "bash scripts/notify-teams.sh --preview"
    }

Then personalize (search and replace):
* saucelabs_request.yml: app_name, owner_email, team, description, repository, business_unit
* bitrise.yml: SAUCE_APP_FILE_NAME, PROJECT_LOCATION, MODULE (Android); BITRISE_PROJECT_PATH, BITRISE_SCHEME (iOS)
* tests/ or .env: SAUCE_REGION, SAUCE_APP_FILENAME_*, SAUCE_DEVICE_*, APP_PACKAGE
* tests/pages/: Your screen's accessibility IDs
Android Release build prerequisite: your android/app/build.gradle release buildType
must sign with the debug keystore and keep minifyEnabled false for test builds.
The pipeline generates the keystore itself.
Check: npm run bootstrap then npm run pipeline:local both finish green on your laptop.

Step 2 — Push the repo to Enterprise Git (~5 min)
git remote add origin https://git.enterprise.com/<group>/<your-app>.git # if not set
git push -u origin main # or master — both supported
Check: project exists on Enterprise Git.

Step 3 — Create the Bitrise app (~10 min, the only console step)
* Add new app under the Mobile Engineering workspace.
* git.enterprise.com project URL. Let Bitrise generate an SSH key and add it as a
  deploy key on the GitLab project (read-only is enough).
* Branch: main (or master).
* Pick "use the bitrise.yml in the repository" (store config in repo). Skip auto-detected workflow.
* Note app URL: app.bitrise.io/app/<APP_SLUG>.
Check: Bitrise app page shows workflows primary, deploy-android, deploy-ios.

Step 4 — Create two API tokens (~5 min)
* Bitrise PAT: Account settings -> Security -> Personal access tokens -> create (set expiry).
* GitLab PAT: git.enterprise.com -> Preferences -> Access tokens -> scope api -> create (set expiry).
Check: both tokens copied somewhere safe (ERPM or password manager), not in repo.

Step 5 — Run provisioning (~5 min)
From your repo root, on VPN:
cp .provision.env.example .provision.env
# edit .provision.env — tokens, app slug, project path, secret values
npm run provision        # DRY-RUN: lists every change, touches nothing
npm run provision:apply  # applies it
* Bitrise: creates/updates Secrets in 4.1 and registers incoming webhook on GitLab.
* GitLab: creates/updates webhook to Bitrise, protects default branch (no direct push,
  maintainers merge, no force-push), turns on merge only if pipeline succeeds.
Optional — prove the chain straight away:
./scripts/provision/provision.sh --apply --trigger-build
Check:
* Bitrise App settings -> Secrets lists names in 4.1 (values hidden)
* GitLab Settings -> Webhooks lists Bitrise URL
* GitLab Settings -> Repository -> Protected branches lists default branch with Allowed to push: No one
* GitLab Settings -> Merge requests has Pipelines must succeed ticked
Then delete .provision.env or revoke the two tokens.

Step 6 — Confirm the Sauce device pool (~5 min)
In Sauce Labs -> Real Devices, confirm a device matching your pattern
(Samsung.* for Android, iPhone.* for iOS) is available in your data center.
Check: at least one matching device shows Available.

Step 7 — Turn on notifications (optional, ~2-5 min)
* Microsoft Teams (card in a channel): in Teams, open channel -> ... -> Workflows ->
  template "Send webhook alerts to a channel" -> pick team + channel -> Add workflow ->
  copy URL. Put in TEAMS_WEBHOOK_URL in .provision.env, run npm run notify:test,
  then npm run provision:apply.
  Classic Office 365 "Incoming Webhook" connectors were retired May 2026 and return 403.
  Only Workflows URLs work; scripts/notify-teams.sh sends Adaptive Card format.
* Email (no code, no secret): Bitrise -> App -> Notifications -> Email: add recipient addresses.
Optional add-on — alerts straight from Sauce Labs via webhooks.

Step 8 — Run the smoke tests
Go to Section 8. When all four pass, you are done.

iOS extra steps:
deploy-ios is in bitrise.yml, but iOS needs signing before it can produce an IPA for Sauce:
1. Upload code signing certificate (.p12) and provisioning profile to Bitrise -> App settings -> Code signing. Never commit them — gitignore blocks *.p12 *.mobileprovision.
2. Set BITRISE_PROJECT_PATH, BITRISE_SCHEME, and BITRISE_DISTRIBUTION_METHOD (default: ad-hoc).
3. Check the Sauce app request step (it is marked "enable once signing is configured").
4. Trigger deploy-ios manually from Bitrise until green, then add a trigger for every merge.
